# Shell scripts keep LF even when checked out on Windows.
#
# Without this, git hands a Windows working tree CRLF copies — and a script whose
# lines end in \r is not a script the box can run. `set -euo pipefail` becomes
# `set -euo pipefail\r`, and bash reports "pipefail: invalid option name", which
# is a confusing way to be told about an invisible character. Copying
# deploy/admin/ up from a Windows checkout hit exactly that.
#
# Deliberately narrow: `* text=auto eol=lf` would renormalise every text file in
# the repository and churn the whole tree. These are the files whose line endings
# actually decide whether something works.
*.sh            text eol=lf
deploy/admin/ptd-panel text eol=lf
